  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` and `src/aura-assets.ts`, and `typecheck` passes. I couldn't run the app or `vite build` here, so nothing has been checked in a browser.\n\n- **Scene:** `/assets/soldier.glb` is scaled to a 1.8 m height and placed on a ground plane. The camera is a fixed perspective camera framing the whole character. One shadow-casting directional light (2048 px shadow map) lights the scene, plus a soft ambient fill.\n- **Animation:** the soldier starts on `Idle`. `R` calls `play(\"Run\", { crossFade: 0.4 })` and `I` calls `play(\"Idle\", { crossFade: 0.4 })`.\n- **Eval hooks:** `window.__EVAL__.clip()` returns the target clip name, switching as soon as a fade starts. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the app is ready, the GLB reports loaded (or errored, or 15 s pass), and two frames have been drawn.\n- **Asset hash is a guess:** `src/aura-assets.ts` is hand-written because the CLI isn't available. The `sha256` and bounds are copied from the engine's bundled soldier fixture, which has the same clips (Idle, Run, TPose, Walk) as `soldier.glb`. I assumed `soldier.glb` is that same file and didn't check. From the engine code I read, the hash only decides whether the asset counts as a typed asset, and I didn't see it being checked against the file.\n- **Crossfade is read from the source, not seen:** I read the engine's animation code and saw it applies a `crossFade` value passed to `play`. I haven't seen the blend happen on screen.\n\n",
